          content: `<h2>Integrating Cultural Humility into Every Clinical Encounter</h2>
<p>Cultural competence in geriatric depression practice is not a supplementary skill applied to clients who appear culturally different from the clinician but a fundamental orientation that shapes every clinical encounter, including those with clients who share the clinician's own racial, ethnic, or cultural background. Every older adult client is a unique individual whose experience of aging, depression, and treatment is shaped by a specific constellation of cultural identities — including the often-invisible cultural assumptions of the dominant culture that may be so taken for granted by both client and clinician as to remain entirely unexamined.</p>
<p>The concept of cultural humility — as distinct from cultural competence — is particularly relevant in geriatric practice. Cultural competence implies the acquisition of a definable body of knowledge about specific cultural groups that can be systematically mastered. Cultural humility instead positions the clinician as a perpetual learner who approaches each client's cultural experience with genuine curiosity and the explicit acknowledgment of the clinician's own cultural limitations and potential biases. The culturally humble clinician does not assume that their knowledge of "Latino culture" or "Asian American culture" accurately predicts any specific client's experience — because within every broad cultural category, the diversity of individual experience, acculturative generation, national origin, regional background, socioeconomic class, and personal history produces enormous variation that no categorical knowledge can fully capture.</p>
<p>Practical cultural humility in geriatric depression assessment involves the explicit invitation to the client to educate the clinician about their own cultural framework: "I want to understand how you make sense of what you've been experiencing. In your family, or in the community where you grew up, what would people say about someone who was feeling the way you've been feeling? What would be the right thing for that person to do?" This open-ended inquiry communicates genuine respect for the client's cultural expertise about their own experience, provides clinical information that no standardized instrument can generate, and establishes the collaborative relational stance that is both clinically effective and ethically appropriate across all cultural contexts.</p>`
